  "query_preview": "..Main player in the weather over this weekend will be\nthe early season trough parked over the West Coast. It's brought\nsome mid and upper level drying which as shown on the 18Z sounding\nputs a pretty strong cap over the western half of the state.  \nDespite having some good low level moisture today which has \nproduced some low elevation cumulus fields, thunderstorms will be \nhard to come by west of I-17 and US-89 for the rest of the \nafternoon. The eastern mountains like the Whites, Chuskas and East\nRim don't have as much capping so some scattered showers and \nthunderstorms will be possible there this afternoon. A similar \nscenario is expected for tomorrow as capping keeps the western \nhalf of the state dry, with more moisture and less capping over \nthe eastern part of the state allowing some showers/storms during \nthe afternoon.\n\nNow for Sunday, how things play out depend on how much moisture\nthe West Coast trough can tap from Hurricane Marie. Right now a\nmoisture plume will extend across southern California into Nevada\nwith the eastern edge moving over the northwestern quarter Arizona,\nincluding the Grand Canyon region. This combined with a shortwave\nwrapping around that trough could bring some heavy rain to this \narea. WPC does have this area in a slight risk for excessive \nrainfall so will have to keep an eye on things to see how much of \na flash flooding threat will materialize. The rest of the state is\nexpected to remain relatively dry as it'll be east of the band of\nmoisture. Some monsoon moisture may remain in far eastern \nArizona, enough for shower/storm chances over the White Mountains.\n\n\nBy Monday this moisture plume will push out of the area and the\nridge of high pressure over the southeastern US will start flexing\nback westward towards us again. This will draw in monsoon moisture\nfor the eastern third of the state bringing that region some\nshower and thunderstorm chances. By Wednesday onward it flexes\nwest enough to bring shower and thunderstorm chances back to most\nof the state.",
